{"key":"F30","report_id":"R0044","kind":"FIELD","label":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver/cvar_bat_subgradient","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"The framework reformulates worst-case channel reliability power minimization via Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR), solves collaborative DNN partitioning and offloading via the Bat-inspired CPOO algorithm, and solves the coupled master problem via the LaCPOO algorithm combining Lagrangian subgradient projection and KKT power updates with CPOO.","page":6,"locator":"Section III.C Joint optimization of two subproblems, paragraph 2","excerpt":"The algorithm iteratively combines subgradient projection with the CPOO method to find feasible solutions.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F31","report_id":"R0044","kind":"FIELD","label":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery/fading_and_bs_fallback","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Ground-to-UAV offloading is designed under worst-case channel fading to ensure transmission reliability, and if an MD fails to receive returned results from all offloading UAVs due to unreliable channels, it transmits the whole task at maximum power to the base station for fallback reprocessing.","page":3,"locator":"Section II.C.2 Offload to BS, paragraph 1","excerpt":"Since the Ground-to-UAV (G2U) transmission has already been considered under the worst-case channel fading, the reliability is largely guaranteed.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F32","report_id":"R0044","kind":"FIELD","label":"evaluation_platform_simulator_hardware_and_traces/simulation_area","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"The simulation models mobile devices randomly distributed in a square network area with a base station at the center and four edge-server UAVs flying clockwise at 10 m/s at a fixed altitude.","page":6,"locator":"Section IV.A Simulation Setup and Pameters, paragraph 1","excerpt":"And flying speed of the UAVs is 10 m/s in a clockwise direction.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F33","report_id":"R0044","kind":"FIELD","label":"baselines_and_workloads/baselines","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"CPOO and LaCPOO are compared against three baselines: Local (all DNN inferences processed locally), RPOM (random partitioning of the last convolutional layer and random subtask offloading), and Edgent-P (nearest-UAV selection with two-part computing-resource-based task division between UAV and local MD).","page":7,"locator":"Section IV.A Simulation Setup and Pameters, baselines list","excerpt":"Each MD selects the nearest UAV, and then divides the DNN-based task into two parts based on computing resources. The former is offloaded to the UAV, while the latter is processed locally.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F34","report_id":"R0044","kind":"FIELD","label":"sensitivity_and_variability/bw_reliability_psi1","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"Sensitivity evaluations on page 7 examine average inference delay and transmission power across varying transmission bandwidth (Fig. 5), offloading reliability threshold (Figs. 6 and 7), and latency weight parameter (Fig. 8), showing delay decreases with higher bandwidth and higher reliability thresholds while transmission power increases with stricter reliability.","page":7,"locator":"Section IV.B Numerical Results, paragraphs 3 and 4, and Figs. 5-8","excerpt":"Fig. 5 depicts the average delay under different bandwidth. As shown in the figure, all the algorithms progressively reduce the average delay as the transmission bandwidth increases.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F35","report_id":"R0045","kind":"FIELD","label":"partition_and_assignment/two_part_split_and_migration","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"An integer partitioning decision divides each user's DNN task into two segments where the first part executes locally on the user device and the second part is offloaded to an associated UAV, including full offloading and full local execution as boundary cases.","page":4,"locator":"Section II.C DNN Partitioning Model, paragraph 2","excerpt":"When the DNN-based task is divided into two parts, the first part is processed locally, and the second part is offloaded onto UAVs for processing.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F36","report_id":"R0045","kind":"FIELD","label":"decision_variables_objectives_constraints_and_solver/sac_per_cvx","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"A hierarchical DRL and convex optimization framework minimizes total weighted energy consumption by using a soft actor-critic with prioritized experience replay (SAC-PER) agent for high-level DNN partitioning, user-UAV association, task migration, and UAV trajectory decisions while embedding a CVX convex optimization subroutine as an inner-loop deterministic solver for UAV computing resource allocation at each time slot.","page":6,"locator":"Section III. PROPOSED APPROACH, paragraph 3","excerpt":"Based on these decisions, the convex computing resource allocation subproblem is constructed and solved via the CO module to obtain the optimal computing resource allocation. In this manner, the CO solver serves as an inner loop deterministic optimizer embedded within each DRL interaction step.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
{"key":"F37","report_id":"R0045","kind":"FIELD","label":"mobility_channel_intermittency_failure_and_recovery/threshold_migration_3d","status":"OBSERVED","finding":"UAVs dynamically adjust their 3-D flight trajectories across horizontal movement and altitude control to approach user-dense areas and conserve flight energy while supporting threshold-triggered inter-UAV task migration.","page":11,"locator":"Section IV.B.3 Trajectory, paragraph 1","excerpt":"During task execution, UAVs dynamically adjust their trajectories according to the spatial distribution of users, actively approaching user-dense areas to perform tasks, which effectively improve the service coverage and resource utilization.","quantitative":null,"reason":""}
